course: dictionary
lesson: 4893
title: Iguanas: lizards that swim
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An iguana is a large lizard with a row of spines down its back. Iguanas live in hot parts of Central and South America and the Caribbean.
explain: Iguanas are reptiles, like other lizards and snakes. They are cold-blooded, so they need the sun to warm up. They have scaly skin and lay eggs.
explain: Most green iguanas are plant eaters. The marine iguana is different: «The marine iguana is the only lizard that swims and feeds in the sea.» Context clue: the word marine means of the sea.
fact: An iguana is a large lizard with a row of spines down its back.
fact: Iguanas live in hot places in Central and South America and the Caribbean.
fact: The marine iguana of the Galapagos Islands is the only lizard that swims and feeds in the sea.
fact: Charles Darwin saw marine iguanas in the Galapagos.
fact: Sally Lightfoot crabs are bright red crabs that leap from rock to rock and pick ticks off marine iguanas.
exercise: What is an iguana? | *A large lizard with a row of spines down its back | A small fish with fins | A bird that cannot fly
exercise: «The marine iguana dives for seaweed.» Where does it find its food? | In the desert | Up in the trees | *In the sea
exercise: Iguanas are reptiles. Which group of animals do they belong to? | Birds | Mammals | Fish | *Reptiles
test: Why do iguanas need the sun? | To make their spines grow | *To warm up, because they are cold-blooded | To find seaweed to eat
test: Which iguana «is the only lizard that swims and feeds in the sea»? | *Marine iguana | Green iguana | Spiny iguana
exam: «Sally Lightfoot crabs pick ticks off marine iguanas.» What do the crabs do for the iguanas? | *Pick ticks off them | Give them food | Keep them warm at night | Take them into the sea
